  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2z} & =3y-2x \\\\ z & =\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}.$",
